  "notes": [
   "Read from the current raster copy (H-1-5294, March 2026, kodak-ektachrome-100d.pdf), the only copy available. The three plots draw each layer in its own colour (red, green, blue), so each line is selected by its ink colour and the hints were placed on the printed lines by locating the coloured ink of the rendered page.",
   "Exposure indexes printed: daylight (5500 K) 100; tungsten (3200 K) 25 with the 80A filter. The curves are for daylight, so balance is daylight and iso 100 (the sheet does not print an exposure time or exposure light on the sensitometric plot).",
   "Characteristic curves (reversal film: density falls as exposure rises): x is the top axis, log exposure in lux-seconds (-4 at the left frame to +1 at the right frame); the bottom axis gives camera stops (-10 to 6). The sheet says '0' on the stops axis is normal exposure of an 18-percent grey card in the red, green and blue layers; that tick sits on the log exposure -1.0 grid line, which is what log_h_ref gives (read from the axes, not a printed number). A white card is 2 1/3 stops above normal and a 3-percent black card 2 2/3 stops below. The curves are drawn from about log exposure -2.8 to +0.5 (stops -6 to +3.5); the layers cross near log exposure -1.6 to -1.4 (density about 2).",
   "Densitometry is not printed on this sheet, so densitometry is null. The dye plot and the granularity data say Process E-6.",
   "Spectral sensitivity: no effective exposure or density criterion is printed; y is log sensitivity as printed (-2.0 to 2.0, a different scale from the negative stocks). The yellow-forming layer (b) is drawn from about 395 to 520 nm, magenta (g) 472 to 603 nm, cyan (r) 554 to 684 nm; the lines stop where Kodak stops them.",
   "Spectral dye density: process E-6, diffuse density, title 'Normalized Dyes to form a visual neutral density of 1.0 for a viewing illuminant of 5000K'. The note says the cyan, magenta and yellow curves are peak-normalised, but as drawn they peak at about 0.92 (yellow, 440 nm), 0.93 (magenta, 550 nm) and 0.94 (cyan, 660 nm), not 1.0; mid is the black Midscale curve (peak about 1.14 at 560 nm). There is no minimum-density curve on this sheet. The yellow dye runs along the 0.0 line of the plot from about 560 to 700 nm. Only 400 to 700 nm is plotted.",
   "Grain: the sheet gives diffuse rms granularity curves only (48 micrometre aperture, Process E-6, exposed with tungsten light filtered to 5500 K; Sigma D scale on the right, multiply by 1000), no single printed value, so grain is null. Read by eye at density 1.0 (log relative exposure about -1.0) Sigma D is roughly 0.013 for the green and red records (rms about 13) and 0.02 for the blue record.",
